{
  "_readme": [
    "WHICH EMOJI LIVE IN WHICH CONTAINER. This is the game's taxonomy.",
    "",
    "RULES (checked at boot — a violation shows an error screen, not a silent bug):",
    "  1. Every emoji must appear in exactly ONE container in this whole file.",
    "     An emoji has one home; two homes makes a run unwinnable.",
    "  2. Every emoji must also have an entry in names.json.",
    "  3. Never list the key or coin emoji here (see tokens in furniture.json) —",
    "     they are tools, not clutter.",
    "  4. 'kind' must exist in furniture.json kinds, and maps to .k-<kind> in css/furniture.css.",
    "  5. 'floor' must exist in furniture.json floors, and maps to .floor-<floor> in css/room.css.",
    "  6. A room may not have more containers than there are anchors in furniture.json.",
    "",
    "THE RULE NO VALIDATOR CAN CHECK — keep the guessable ones together.",
    "If a reasonable person could put an emoji in either of two containers, the",
    "game is asking them to read the designer's mind, and being wrong costs a",
    "trip across the house. So: confusable things share a container, and the",
    "container's NAME is the rule.",
    "This file used to fail that badly. The Seed Rack held a cactus and a potted",
    "plant while the Garden Basket held flowers and leaves — both are 'plants',",
    "and nothing on screen said which was which. The Fridge held lettuce and",
    "cucumber (also the Produce Crate's business) plus three berries (also the",
    "Fruit Bowl's). Soap sat in the Vanity while its twin the sponge sat under",
    "the sink. Each of those is now one container with a name that states the",
    "test: Vegetable Crate takes vegetables, the Fridge takes cold dairy, the",
    "Potting Bench takes what's growing in a pot.",
    "Before adding an emoji, say out loud which container it goes in and why. If",
    "the sentence needs an 'although', it's in the wrong place.",
    "",
    "'short' is what fits on the furniture face; 'name' is used in titles and messages.",
    "'sizeFactor' scales this room relative to the others (1 = biggest)."
  ],
